    description: "Parent: WI-razub-duluf-nobun-rulit-dapam-jipal-dafud-nahob (shipped mechanism lacks the \"meta-circuit-breaker\" the original design flagged).\n\n## Motivation\n\nThe agent-supervisor daemon (WI-rofuv) has ONE fork-bomb protection today: a soft 24h rate limit (default 8 spawns). That's rolling-window throttling, not pattern detection.\n\n**The real failure mode this doesn't cover.** A persistent bug — broken playbook, corrupt state file, bad env var — doesn't get *caught* by the 24h cap, it gets *absorbed* by it. A workstation stuck in a persistent-failure loop produces exactly this pattern:\n\n```\nDay 1:  8 useless spawns over ~8 min, then silence for ~23h52m\nDay 2:  same: 8 useless spawns at day-start, then silence\nDay 3:  same\n...\n```\n\nNothing alerts. The daemon dutifully rate-limits itself each morning and appears healthy from the outside. The kill switch this item adds is what turns that silent state into a loud \"supervisor auto-paused, investigate\" state — a persistent bug stops being invisible.\n\nThe WI-razub design doc called this out as explicit **open question #2** (\"Anti-fork-bomb / intelligent breaker\"). The \"Roads not taken\" list dropped chain-length tracking in the interest of shipping, but the general concern was not resolved. Raised again by the human on 2026-04-18 in direct discussion.\n\n## The real signal, in one sentence\n\n**The kill switch fires on \"N consecutive no-progress failures on the same chain\" — time-agnostic.** Whether those N failures take five minutes or five hours, the pattern is the same: the supervisor has tried and tried again and nothing is getting done. That's what auto-pause responds to.\n\n## Terminology\n\nMatch the existing vocabulary in `.agent/hooks/_shared/stop_logic.sh`, which calls the circuit-breaker trip signal \"no progress\":\n\n* **No-progress failure** — at replacement time, the dying session's pane-byte delta from spawn-to-kill was below a small threshold (proposal: 512 bytes). The CLI rendered almost nothing before dying. Increments the chain's consecutive-failure counter.\n* **Progress replacement** — pane-byte delta above the threshold. The CLI did real work before getting stuck. Resets the chain's consecutive-failure counter.\n\nDo NOT use \"productive/unproductive\" — it's semantically backwards (the no-progress case is the UNproductive one, not the productive one).\n\n## Proposal\n\nThree pieces. Only piece (3) is the actual signal; pieces (1)–(2) are scaffolding that exists to make the signal fireable.\n\n### 1. Chain-length tracking in meta.json (scaffolding)\n\nWhen the supervisor replaces a session, the new session's `meta.json` records `replaces: <old-session-id>` and `chain_length: <prior_chain_length + 1>`. An initial spawn (no predecessor) has `chain_length: 1`.\n\n**What this enables.** Gives the supervisor the memory it needs for pieces (2) and (3). Without chain-length tracking, 30 unrelated spawns over a day look the same as 30 consecutive respawns on a broken chain.\n\n### 2. No-progress failure classification (scaffolding)\n\nA replacement is a **no-progress failure** if the dying session's pane-byte delta from spawn-to-kill was ≤ 512 bytes. Above the threshold is a **progress replacement**.\n\nA no-progress failure increments the chain's consecutive-no-progress counter. A progress replacement resets it to 0. That counter is the input to the kill switch.\n\n### 3. Consecutive-failure kill switch (the actual signal)\n\nAfter **5 consecutive no-progress failures on the same chain** — no time limit — the supervisor writes `supervisor.auto-paused` into its state directory and stops spawning entirely until the operator clears it. Logs a prominent message to `respawn_log.log` with the chain tail.\n\nThis is the mechanism that says \"I've failed five times in a row on this chain, I'm not going to keep trying.\" A persistent bug goes from invisible-daily-cap-absorption to a visible auto-paused state that the operator sees in `agent-supervisor status`.\n\n## Explicitly NOT doing\n\n- **Short-window fast-fire cooldown.** The earlier draft of this item proposed a \"3+ spawns in 10 minutes → 30 minute cooldown\" check. Dropped after analysis showed the kill switch fires on the chain counter *before* any plausible cooldown would trigger (the kill-switch sequence completes in ~5 minutes at the default 60s poll interval), AND a 30-minute cooldown is a *shorter* breather than the existing 24h rate limit, so the cooldown would actually allow MORE resource waste per day than doing nothing. The short-window idea doesn't add value; the kill switch alone is the mechanism that matters.\n- Auto-reporting / alerting the operator via non-file channels (email, Slack, desktop notification). The state file + log are enough for operators who run `agent-supervisor status`.\n- Cross-project chain tracking. Each project's supervisor has its own state directory; we don't correlate chains across workstations or repos.\n\n## Tests\n\n- Chain-length tracking increments correctly across a 3-replace sequence.\n- `replaces` pointer is written to the new session's meta.json and points at the old session-id.\n- No-progress threshold: ≤ 512 bytes delta → no-progress failure; > 512 bytes → progress replacement.\n- Kill switch fires at the 5th consecutive no-progress failure.\n- Kill switch does NOT fire when a progress replacement interrupts the chain (counter resets).\n- Kill switch IS time-agnostic — 5 failures over 24h trigger it the same as 5 failures in 5 minutes.\n- `supervisor.auto-paused` file blocks future spawns until cleared.\n- Clearing `supervisor.auto-paused` (via the new `resume` subcommand) resets the chain counters.\n- Attached-client check still takes precedence over the kill switch (human-in-the-loop escape hatch).\n- Decision matrix: `autonomous_intent=OFF` short-circuits before the meta-breaker logic runs.\n\n## Operator UX\n\n- `agent-supervisor status` surfaces: chain-length per session, consecutive-no-progress count, whether auto-pause is active.\n- `agent-supervisor resume` clears `supervisor.auto-paused` and resets the chain counters. (New subcommand — small addition to the argparse tree.)\n- `docs/agent-supervisor.md` operator guide picks up the new `status` fields, the `resume` subcommand, and the fault patterns the meta-breaker guards against.\n\n## Governance\n\nTouches `scripts/agent-supervisor` (non-governance per the AGENTS.md list) and `docs/agent-supervisor.md`. Adds config knobs but the defaults are safe; no existing user behavior changes unless a persistent failure actually occurs. Does NOT touch `.agent/hooks/**` or `AGENTS.md` (except a small pointer update from the existing Vendor Parity section if helpful) so no human approval is required beyond the scope of this ticket itself."  # 94ca
- op: discuss  # 164e
